<section>
  <grid spacing="none">
    <grid-item>
      <p role="eyebrow">Three-era comparison · Dominant tendencies</p>
      <h2>What changes across the three eras?</h2>
      <table borders="all" striped="false" col-widths="[16,28,28,28]">
        <tr>
          <th>Dimension</th>
          <th background-color="palette-2">Print · Fixed instance</th>
          <th background-color="palette-3">Digital · Coded state</th>
          <th background-color="accent">Computational · Generated variation</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Production</th>
          <td valign="middle">Composed instance</td>
          <td valign="middle">Coded state</td>
          <td valign="middle">Generated variation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Distribution</th>
          <td valign="middle">Editions and physical circulation</td>
          <td valign="middle">Network access and maintained versions</td>
          <td valign="middle">On-demand synthesis</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Audience</th>
          <td valign="middle">Reader</td>
          <td valign="middle">User</td>
          <td valign="middle">Participant and evaluator</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Authorship</th>
          <td valign="middle">Visible makers</td>
          <td valign="middle">Collaborative technical stacks</td>
          <td valign="middle">People, data, models, and interfaces</td>
        </tr>
      </table>
      <aside variant="note">
        <p>These are overlapping analytical lenses, not sealed eras. Each newer system carries older forms—and their assumptions—inside it.</p>
      </aside>
    </grid-item>
  </grid>
</section>